    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ 04 THE CEILING OF RULES, the pivot ══ -->
  <section class="bg-[var(--color-surface)] sec-y" data-reveal>
    <div class="container-pg">
      <SectionLabel icon="judgment">04. Why rules were not enough</SectionLabel>
      <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-4 max-w-2xl">
        Eight flags. Every one a symptom, none a cause.
      </h2>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-10">
        The first version was a rule engine and it works. It is deterministic and
        auditable. But a year of real reviews exposed a ceiling: it tells you <em>what</em>
        happened and structurally cannot tell you <em>why</em>.
      </p>

      <div class="flags mb-10">
        {["Overdue", "Scope Changed", "Blocked", "No Update 3w+", "Missing PRD", "Due Soon", "Stale 2w+", "Dates Drifted"]
          .map(f => <span class="flag">{f}</span>)}
      </div>

      <div class="split">
        <div class="split-col">
          <p class="split-k">What the rule says</p>
          <p class="split-t">“Dates Drifted”</p>
          <p class="split-b">
            This ticket's committed date moved three times. That is a true statement
            derived from the changelog. It is also where the information stops.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="split-col split-col--alt">
          <p class="split-k">What I actually needed to know</p>
          <p class="split-t">Why it moved three times</p>
          <p class="split-b">
            The PRD changed twice and the third move was a designer quietly absorbing
            someone else's work. One is a product problem and one is a capacity problem.
            They need different conversations. The answer was in the comment thread and
            the changelog, which no threshold can read.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ 05 THE AI LAYER ══ -->
  <section class="sec-y" data-reveal>
    <div class="container-pg">
      <SectionLabel icon="impact">05. The AI layer (prototype)</SectionLabel>
      <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-4 max-w-2xl">
        Rules decide what's true. AI explains what it means.
      </h2>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-12">
        AI never decides whether something is at risk. It reads what the rules have
        already flagged and adds judgement and language on top. That ordering is the whole
        architecture. The rule engine below ships and runs weekly; the AI layer is a working
        prototype on the same live data, with the model call stubbed.
      </p>

      <figure class="pipe" data-reveal>
        <div class="pipe-flow">
          <div class="pipe-node">
            <p class="pipe-n-t">Jira</p>
            <p class="pipe-n-s">System of record</p>
          </div>
          <span class="pipe-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <div class="pipe-node">
            <p class="pipe-n-t">Deterministic rules</p>
            <p class="pipe-n-s">Same input, same output, every run</p>
          </div>
          <span class="pipe-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <div class="pipe-node">
