  <!-- ══ 06 THE CLOSED LOOP ══ -->
  <section class="bg-[var(--color-surface)] sec-y" data-reveal>
    <div class="container-pg">
      <SectionLabel icon="validated">06. Closing the loop</SectionLabel>
      <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-4 max-w-3xl">A recommendation nobody measures is just a confident opinion.</h2>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-10">
        The system could explain a slip and propose a move, then nothing. No record of
        what was decided and no way to know whether it helped. So I built the rest:
        decision, approved write-back, and verification against a baseline captured at
        decision time.
      </p>

      <ScreenGallery
        id="loop-screens"
        screens={[
          {
            src: `${IMG}/loop-decide.webp`,
            name: "Decide",
            alt: "A ticket panel showing the AI explanation followed by a decision bar offering accept, investigate or dismiss, with a note that nothing is written to Jira until the exact record is approved",
            caption: "the recommendation now ends in a decision: accept, investigate or dismiss, with a reason recorded against the pattern.",
          },
          {
            src: `${IMG}/loop-approve.webp`,
            name: "Approve the write",
            alt: "An approval step listing precisely what will be created: the ticket key and title, the owner, what it links to, and the body text, with approve and cancel buttons",
            caption: "the only step that writes. It shows the exact record first: key, owner, link and body. Nothing happens on silence.",
          },
          {
            src: `${IMG}/loop-log.webp`,
            name: "Verify",
            alt: "A decision log panel listing three past decisions with the baseline captured at decision time and a computed verdict for each: one slipped further, one held, one still monitoring",
            caption: "every decision with its baseline, and whether the signal actually moved. Including the one where the intervention failed.",
          },
        ]}
      />

      <div class="cards cards--3 mt-12">
        <div class="card">
          <p class="card-k">Rule 01</p>
          <p class="card-t">Nothing writes without approval</p>
          <p class="card-b">
            No hidden side effects and every write is undoable. It creates a new linked
            record and never edits a ticket's own fields.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="card-k">Rule 02</p>
          <p class="card-t">The baseline is captured, not remembered</p>
          <p class="card-b">
            At decision time the system records the signal as of that date from the
            changelog. Verification compares against that record and not against anyone's
            memory.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="card-k">Rule 03</p>
          <p class="card-t">It must be able to say it was wrong</p>
          <p class="card-b">
            A verdict is only worth something if the other verdict was possible. Failed
            interventions are reported in the same voice as successful ones.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="quote max-w-3xl mt-12">
        Building this forced a fix I would have missed. The first version declared “Held”
        the moment a decision was made because nothing had moved yet. A system that reports
        its own success before any time has passed is worse than one that stays quiet. It
        now says “too early to judge” and waits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ 07 WHAT I CHOSE NOT TO BUILD ══ -->
  <section class="sec-y" data-reveal>
    <div class="container-pg">
      <SectionLabel icon="judgment">07. What I chose not to build</SectionLabel>
      <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-4 max-w-3xl">The hard part isn't the AI. It's the data you don't have.</h2>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-8">
        It is easy to draw a product with a design-health score on it. It is harder to say
        where that number comes from. I sorted every signal I wanted by whether the data
        behind it actually exists. That cut the concept by half and made what survived
        defensible.
      </p>

      <p class="text-[var(--color-ink-2)] leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-10">
        <span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Derivable now and shipped:</span>
        delivery risk, capacity load, workflow bottleneck, scope deviation and skipped
        validation. All of them come from the Jira changelog and recorded effort.
      </p>

      <div class="sig">
        {[
          ["Design-system deviation", "Needs component adoption data from Figma. Meaningful, but a second integration and a second data model.", "Needs a source", "v-src"],
          ["Alignment risk", "Needs initiatives linked to product priorities and engineering commitments. That link rarely exists without deliberate tagging.", "Needs a source", "v-src"],
          ["Research coverage", "Needs a repository with initiatives tagged to studies. Rare and rarely current.", "Needs a source", "v-src"],
          ["Strategic vs reactive mix", "There is no field for this. A person has to classify intent per initiative. That is a process change and not a feature.", "Not without process change", "v-no"],
          ["Design debt", "No agreed definition and no consistent tagging. Any number here would be invented so the system doesn't offer one.", "Not without process change", "v-no"],
        ].map(([n, d, v, cls]) => (
          <div class="sig-r">
            <p class="sig-n">{n}</p>
            <p class="sig-d">{d}</p>
            <span class={`sig-v ${cls}`}>{v}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="quote max-w-3xl mt-12">
        Cutting these five is the part of this work I would defend hardest. A system that
        invents two of its numbers cannot be trusted on the other eight.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ 08 HOW IT WAS BUILT ══ -->
  <section class="bg-[var(--color-surface)] sec-y" data-reveal>
    <div class="container-pg">
      <SectionLabel icon="craft">08. How it was built</SectionLabel>
      <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-4 max-w-2xl">Constraints chosen on purpose.</h2>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-12">
        No spec, no backend and no budget for tooling. Three decisions I'd defend rather
        than apologise for.
      </p>

      <div class="cards cards--3">
        <div class="card">
          <p class="card-k">Decision 01</p>
          <p class="card-t">No framework, no build step</p>
          <p class="card-b">
            Every screen is rendered by hand-written template functions. Anyone on the
            team runs it with one command. No CI, no deploy pipeline and no dependency
            rot on an internal tool nobody is paid to maintain.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="card-k">Decision 02</p>
          <p class="card-t">No database</p>
          <p class="card-b">
            State lives in memory and is overwritten by each Jira sync. It is snapshotted
            to localStorage so a reload never reverts to demo data. Single-machine is a
            real tradeoff taken for zero infrastructure cost.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="card-k">Decision 03</p>
          <p class="card-t">The smallest possible backend</p>
          <p class="card-b">
            Jira's API won't accept browser calls. An 83-line local proxy replays the
            request server-side and the token never leaves the machine. A three-step
            wizard fuzzy-matches each org's own field names so the tool is portable
            rather than personal.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="cs-label mt-16 mb-4">What actually took the time</p>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-8">
        The bug was never where the symptom was. Each of these started as a number that read
        wrong, and building it myself meant following it back to the assumption underneath.
      </p>
      <div class="rows">
        {[
          ["A frozen “today”", "The current date was a hardcoded constant that never updated. Nothing crashed. Every date calculation quietly reported “on track” forever. Found from an offhand comment about one overdue ticket."],
          ["The same bug, found three times", "“Status says at risk, detail shows nothing” appeared in three views that each derived risk from a different definition. Seeing one problem and not three fixes kept it from coming back."],
          ["Pagination double-counting", "Syncing pages sorted by last-updated meant a ticket edited mid-sync could shift between pages and be counted twice. Found from a user saying “Jira says 74, this shows 84” and not from any error log."],
        ].map(([t, d]) => (
          <div class="row">
            <p class="row-t">{t}</p>
            <p class="row-d">{d}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="cs-label mt-16 mb-4">Rules I held to</p>
      <div class="cards cards--3">
        {[
          ["Auto-create from real data", "Never hardcode an allowlist. Whoever Jira says is assigned gets created. A fixed seed list breaks the moment real usage diverges from demo data."],
          ["Honest by default", "No fabricated history: a workload heatmap was cut because Jira dates change constantly and any reconstruction would be untrustworthy. Empty states say what to map in Settings and not a wall of zeros."],
          ["Report, don't edit", "The ticket panel is read-only with one “Open in Jira” link. One system of record and not two."],
        ].map(([t, d]) => (
          <div class="card">
            <p class="card-t">{t}</p>
            <p class="card-b">{d}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══ 09 OUTCOME AND REFLECTION ══ -->
  <section class="sec-y" data-reveal>
    <div class="container-pg">
      <SectionLabel icon="impact">09. What it changed</SectionLabel>
      <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-4 max-w-2xl">An operations problem, solved with a dashboard.</h2>
      <p class="cs-lede max-w-2xl mb-12">
        The dashboard didn't make the weekly rebuild faster. It removed the need for it.
      </p>

      <div class="stat-grid">
        <div class="stat">
          <p class="stat-n">[X hrs / week]</p>
          <p class="stat-l">Saved from manual exports</p>
          <p class="stat-s">Eliminated the CSV pull and pivot table rebuild before every weekly review.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <p class="stat-n">[X days earlier]</p>
          <p class="stat-l">Slips caught before the review</p>
          <p class="stat-s">Flagged as commitments move, not discovered in the meeting where it's already too late to act.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <p class="stat-n">[X designers / X pods]</p>
          <p class="stat-l">Working from one shared number</p>
          <p class="stat-s">Leads and designers read the same board instead of reconciling competing spreadsheets.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="max-w-2xl mt-20">
        <SectionLabel icon="judgment">Reflection</SectionLabel>
        <h2 class="cs-h2 mb-8">What building it taught me.</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6 text-[var(--color-ink-2)] leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I've spent years on the design side of the triad and for most of that time the
            build happened after I was done. Here it didn't. What that changed was my sense
            of where the cost sits. Most of the elapsed time wasn't layout or visual design.
            It was making sure two views couldn't disagree and that a number meant what it
            appeared to mean. I had always known that work existed. I ask for it
            differently now.
          </p>
          <p>
            I built it in Claude Code which is worth saying plainly. Prototyping at that
            speed is why this exists at all. The version of me who had to file a ticket and
            wait for engineering capacity would never have got past the spreadsheet. It was
            not a substitute for knowing which problem was worth solving or deciding what to
            cut.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

